# Helm Architecture Rules

## Chart Structure

Every chart must contain all of the following:
- `Chart.yaml` — name, description, version, appVersion
- `values.yaml` — defaults; every value has an inline comment explaining its purpose
- `templates/` — Kubernetes manifests as Go templates
- `NOTES.txt` — post-install instructions telling the user how to access and verify the release

Optional but strongly recommended: `values-dev.yaml`, `values-staging.yaml`, `values-prod.yaml`.

## `_helpers.tpl`

Define all reusable template helpers in `templates/_helpers.tpl`. Required helpers:

```
{{- define "chart.fullname" -}}   → release name + chart name, truncated to 63 chars
{{- define "chart.name" -}}       → chart name from .Chart.Name
{{- define "chart.chart" -}}      → chart name + version for helm.sh/chart label
{{- define "chart.labels" -}}     → standard Helm labels (4 labels, see Labels section)
{{- define "chart.selectorLabels" -}} → app.kubernetes.io/name + app.kubernetes.io/instance
```

Use `{{ include "chart.fullname" . }}` for ALL resource names. Never hardcode the release name in a template file.

## Values Layering

- `values.yaml` — safe defaults for local/dev use. All keys documented with comments.
- `values-{env}.yaml` — environment-specific overrides. Must only override; never introduce new undocumented keys.
- Never put secrets (passwords, API keys, tokens) in any values file. Use Sealed Secrets or External Secrets Operator.

## Resource Naming

All Kubernetes resources use `{{ include "chart.fullname" . }}` as the name (or as a prefix for sub-resources). This prevents name collision when multiple releases of the same chart are installed in the same namespace.

## Labels

Every resource's `metadata.labels` must include all four standard Helm labels, generated by `{{ include "chart.labels" . }}`:

```yaml
helm.sh/chart: {{ include "chart.chart" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ include "chart.name" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}
app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: {{ .Release.Service }}
```

Selector labels (used in `spec.selector.matchLabels` and `spec.template.metadata.labels`) use only the two stable labels from `{{ include "chart.selectorLabels" . }}`:
```yaml
app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ include "chart.name" . }}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}
```

Do NOT use `helm.sh/chart` in selector labels — it changes on chart version bump and would break rolling updates.

## Requests and Limits

Every container in every Deployment (and StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job, CronJob) must have resource requests AND limits defined. No unbounded pods.

```yaml
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"
```

These values must be configurable via `values.yaml`. The defaults above are reasonable starting points; production values should be measured and tuned.

## Liveness vs Readiness Probes

Every Deployment must have both `livenessProbe` and `readinessProbe`. They serve different purposes:

- **`readinessProbe`**: controls traffic routing. Failing readiness removes the pod from Service endpoints. Use for "is this pod ready to serve requests?" Use a lighter endpoint (e.g., `/ready` or `/healthz`).
- **`livenessProbe`**: controls pod restart. Failing liveness kills and restarts the container. Use for "is this pod alive or stuck?" Use an endpoint that confirms the app is not deadlocked. Add `initialDelaySeconds` long enough for the app to start.

Different endpoints for liveness and readiness are strongly recommended. Using the same endpoint for both is acceptable only if a single endpoint can distinguish "not yet ready" from "stuck and needs restart."

## ApplicationSet for Multi-Environment

Use ArgoCD ApplicationSet to generate per-environment Applications from a single template. Do not maintain separate Application manifests for dev, staging, and prod — they will drift.

```yaml
# ApplicationSet generates Applications for each environment
generators:
  - list:
      elements:
        - env: dev
          namespace: myapp-dev
        - env: staging
          namespace: myapp-staging
        - env: prod
          namespace: myapp-prod
```
